\description{
R implementation of the Stata \code{table1_tc} command. Summarises variables,
optionally by group, with one row per continuous or binary variable and a
label row plus indented level rows for each categorical variable.
}
\details{
The result is a \code{tt_table}, which prints as a console table. The \code{xlsx},
\code{csv}, and \code{markdown} arguments also write it to a file, and
\code{\link[=tt_write_xlsx]{tt_write_xlsx()}}, \code{\link[flextable:as_flextable]{flextable::as_flextable()}} or \code{\link[=tt_as_gt]{tt_as_gt()}} can write or
convert it later. Argument names mirror the Stata options, so a Stata call
translates option for option.

Descriptive statistics, cell text, row structure, headers, and styling
reproduce Stata tabtools 2.1.14 exactly. Hypothesis tests use R's
conventional tests instead of Stata's: Welch's t-test (two groups) or
Welch's one-way ANOVA (more) for \code{contn} and \code{contln} (on the log scale),
the Wilcoxon rank-sum or Kruskal-Wallis test for \code{conts}, Pearson's
chi-squared test without continuity correction for \code{cat} and \code{bin}, and
Fisher's exact test for \code{cate} and \code{bine}. \code{test_args} passes arguments to
those functions, e.g. \code{list(t.test = list(var.equal = TRUE))} for Stata's
pooled-variance t-test. Fisher's exact test starts from R's default
workspace and enlarges it up to \code{2e7}; when the exact test still cannot
run (large tables), the p-value is simulated from 100,000 Monte Carlo
replicates under a fixed internal seed, so the result is reproducible and
the session's random-number stream is untouched. The same p-value is
then given in every session; it still carries Monte Carlo error (a 95\%
margin of about 0.0014 at p = 0.05). Such rows read
\verb{Fisher's exact (simulated)} in the Test column, the methods paragraph
names the Monte Carlo p-value, and \code{stored$fisher_simulated} lists the
variables.

As in Stata, an empty string is a missing value (in \code{by} and in
categorical variables alike), and a factor level \code{""} is dropped. Date and
date-time columns are refused: Stata counts days from 1960 and R from
1970, so their summaries could not match; convert them first. So are
complex columns, whose imaginary parts would otherwise be discarded.
Also refused are
\code{Inf}, \code{-Inf}, \code{NaN}, and values of 2^1023 (about 8.99e307) or more,
which a Stata variable cannot hold; use \code{NA} for a missing value. Very
large values use Stata's e-notation for the mean (\code{2.e+200}). An
unavailable raw second moment or required sum can leave the SD missing
(\code{.}) or a cell, p-value, or SMD blank. R retains a finite SD when the
square of the weighted centered-deviation sum overflows but its variance
correction remains finite; this deliberately differs from pinned Stata.
SMD uses the unscaled sums and is blank where they overflow.

\code{open} needs a resolved workbook, and \code{mdappend} needs
\code{markdown}. Unlike Stata, \code{title}, \code{footnote}, and \code{borderstyle} work
without a file: the table keeps them for its later destinations
(\code{\link[flextable:as_flextable]{flextable::as_flextable()}}, \code{\link[=tt_as_gt]{tt_as_gt()}}, \code{\link[=tt_write_xlsx]{tt_write_xlsx()}},
\code{\link[=tt_write_markdown]{tt_write_markdown()}}). Stata refuses \code{title()} without \code{excel()} or
\code{markdown()} and \code{borderstyle()} without \code{excel()} only because a Stata
table has nowhere else to go.

\strong{Weights.} \code{wt} names an importance (e.g. IPTW) weight: records with a
missing or zero weight leave the sample and a negative weight is an error.
Means, SDs (Stata \verb{[aw=]}), weighted quantiles, and percentages use the
weight; the \verb{N=} row counts records and an "Effective sample size" row
(Kish's \verb{(sum w)^2 / sum w^2}) follows it. Weighted quartiles do not
depend on the scale of the weights: a weight total below 1 is multiplied
by a power of two first, as in Stata tabtools 2.1.14 (2.1.12's absolute tolerance of 1e-10 returned the two smallest values'
mean for every quartile under weights of 1e-12).
P-values, tests, and the methods
paragraph are dropped, and cells show percentages only unless \code{wtn}
(effective counts as \verb{n (\%)}) or \code{percent_n} is set. \code{wtcompare} puts the
crude table (\code{nopvalue}, no SMD) beside the weighted one, with the SMD from
the weighted pass. \code{fweight} names a frequency weight: records with a
missing or zero weight leave the sample, negative or non-integer weights
are errors, and every count, statistic, and test is on the expanded data.

\strong{Small cells.} \code{smallcells = k} protects every count block against exact
disclosure (a port of Stata's certified engine): counts below \code{k} show as
\verb{<k}, complementary cells as a greater-than-or-equal sign and \code{k},
percentages are withheld for a variable carrying a suppressed count, its
p-value, test, statistic, and SMD read \code{Suppressed}, and a standard note
joins the footnote. As in Stata, the engine protects the counts the table
shows, and also the ones that follow from them (Stata 2.1.17): a
categorical variable's hidden missing count is N minus its shown level
counts, and a binary variable's hidden negative and missing counts follow
from the denominator its n (\%) cell releases. A hidden count below \code{k} is
protected as a primary cell, as if \code{missingsummary} or \code{slashN} printed it:
shown cells get a greater-than-or-equal marker where needed, percentages
are withheld, and the p-value is suppressed. The group and total Ns are
shared by every variable, so in a table of two or more variables they are
never withheld as complementary cells; a count that could only be
protected by withholding them is refused with an error of class
\code{tabtools_error_smallcells}. \code{smallcells} cannot be combined with
percent-only display, which includes \code{wtcompare} without \code{wtn} or
\code{percent_n}.
Count totals, artificial flow bounds, and required flows must be below
2^53 for small-cell certification to preserve single-count changes;
larger frequency-weighted blocks are refused with \code{smallcells}.

\code{smallcells_mode = "primary"} masks only positive printed counts below the
threshold, including printed missing counts, group/total Ns and slash
denominators. Zeros stay visible. Other percentages, tests and SMDs stay as
computed; no complementary protection against reconstruction is promised.
A continuous summary whose contributing N is not printed stays visible.
Weighted effective sample size linked to a masked sample N is still withheld
and counts as a linked display mask, including in primary mode.
Primary mode retains analytical aggregates in \code{stored$raw$table} and
\code{stored$raw$sample_accounting}; publication ledger attachments are redacted.
These raw aggregates are for analysis, not sharing as a protected table.
Active strict masking never creates a raw backup, even if no cells are small.

\strong{Publication replacements.} \code{cellreplace} takes structured R records,
rather than Stata's command-language string. Row labels and column headers
match exactly and case-sensitively after trimming leading/trailing spaces;
ambiguous or missing matches are errors. Numeric column 1 means the first
value column, excluding the row-label column; character \code{"1"} means that
exact header. The final printed order, including Total/crude/weighted and
statistical columns, determines positions. The descriptor/N row and ESS
row can also be selected by their printed row labels. Every record is
checked before any replacement or output; repeated targets apply in order,
last text wins. Empty text and quotes, dollar signs, backticks and
backslashes are literal cell content.

Protected cells and withheld linked percentages/denominators cannot be
replaced (\code{tabtools_error_cellreplace_protected}), including a visible count
whose percentage was withheld under strict masking. This is a safety
difference from native Stata's unchecked replacement. Replacement never
interprets numeric-looking text as an estimate. Direct p/SMD replacements
invalidate the corresponding numeric row/stored value; replacing a variable's
group/Total, test or statistic cell invalidates that variable's publication
p/SMD companions, including a category child's analytical parent. Other
variables' inference remains intact. Replaced summaries invalidate the
affected variable/pass/scope publication ledger; replacing a descriptor N
invalidates only its \code{reported_n}, and replacing a published ESS only its
\code{effective_n}. Permitted unmasked/primary analytical aggregates remain under
\code{stored$raw}; active strict suppression has no such backup. Table 1 does not
support \code{\link[=as_forest_data]{as_forest_data()}}, including after replacement.

\code{desctab()} is an alias of \code{table1_tc()}, as in Stata, where
\code{table1_tc} runs \code{desctab}: it takes the same arguments (\code{data},
\code{vars}, \code{by}, and the rest passed on through \code{...}) and returns the
same table.
}
\section{Session destinations}{

An explicitly supplied non-NULL \code{sheet} enables inherited workbook and
Markdown destinations from \code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}}. Default or NULL sheet does
not request inherited output. Explicit paths still export, and explicit NULL
destinations opt out. A requested sheet without a workbook gives
\code{tabtools_warning_sheet_without_workbook} before output; \code{options(warn = 2)}
interrupts the call. See \code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}} for append/history behavior.
}

\examples{
d <- data.frame(arm = rep(c("A", "B"), each = 25),
                age = c(seq(40, 64), seq(45, 69)),
                sex = factor(rep(c("F", "M", "F", "F", "M"), 10)),
                smoker = rep(c(0, 1), 25))
attr(d$age, "label") <- "Age (years)"
table1_tc(d, by = "arm", vars = c(age = "contn \%5.1f", sex = "cat", smoker = "bin"))

# With SMDs, written to an Excel sheet: the table is then returned
# invisibly, so assign it and print it
xlsx <- tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx")
tab <- table1_tc(d, by = "arm", vars = c(age = "conts", sex = "cat"), smd = TRUE,
                 xlsx = xlsx, sheet = "Table 1")
tab
# A methods paragraph for the paper
tab$stored$methods

# Three groups: balance over all of them, not just the first two
d3 <- data.frame(arm = rep(c("A", "B", "C"), each = 20),
                 age = c(seq(40, 59), seq(41, 60), seq(55, 74)))
table1_tc(d3, by = "arm", vars = c(age = "contn"), smd = TRUE, smdtype = "population")

# desctab() is the same command
desctab(d, by = "arm", vars = c("age", "sex"))
table1_tc(d, by = "arm", vars = c(age = "contn"),
          cellreplace = list(list(row = "Age (years)", column = 1L, text = "Not reported")))
}
